[size=5][b]Crimson Desert Telemetry[/b][/size]
[b]Live light, player, camera and ambient data, with all-around light capture, a 3D radar, fullscreen markers and local HTTP/WebSocket APIs.[/b]

Telemetry reads current light contributions before the renderer's view selection, including sources behind the camera. Optional physics queries estimate collision on camera-to-light paths. Raw data remains available independently of that estimate.

[b]Supports Crimson Desert patch 2.03.02 on Steam for Windows (build 25477059).[/b]

[size=4][b]New in 2.2.5: optional CrimsonHue integration[/b][/size]
Install [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonHue]CrimsonHue 1.0.0[/url] separately for Philips Hue Entertainment lighting and an interactive in-game mixer. [b]CDT works independently; the Hue controller is optional and is not included in this ZIP.[/b]

CDT draws the Hue mixer in its existing D3D12 renderer. CrimsonHue owns bridge communication, protected settings, spatial calibration and lighting output, without a second graphics-hook set. Raw telemetry and the neutral API remain unchanged.

[list]
[*][b]Lighting / Room / Setup / Advanced:[/b] bridge and Entertainment-area setup, ordinary and segmented wall-light calibration, Recommended/Custom controls, presets and diagnostic data.[/*]
[*][b]Lighting weights:[/b] Point lights 0-200%, Spotlights 0-1000%, Own lantern 0-100%. Point/spot is independent of standalone/grouped contributions. Own-lantern matching uses a conservative complete-pair signature for the supported build; missing or ambiguous matches keep ordinary type weighting.[/*]
[*][b]Consistent hiding:[/b] Ctrl+Shift+H, Hide settings and title-bar X hide the mixer while lighting sync continues. Use Stop to end sync, and wait for stopped status before game exit.[/*]
[*][b]Input and cursor corrections:[/b] held delivered game presses finish releasing before panel capture; panel-owned releases finish on hiding. Raw mouse motion has a fallback, and the duplicate native cursor is suppressed inside the owned foreground game client. Release held keys/buttons if prompted.[/*]
[*][b]Room controls:[/b] Point/Wall setup, per-segment wall-washer calibration including horizontal arrangements, and sharper Recommended spatial defaults. Refresh changed Entertainment layouts before restarting sync.[/*]
[/list]

The input changes have mocked-input/shared-renderer coverage; not every independently polled or buffered game-input route has been proven. Neither module starts physical lighting just by loading.

[size=4][b]Telemetry features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]All-around light capture:[/b] current engine lights remain available beyond the camera view. Particle groups are decoded into summed contributions; one physical fire can still produce several contributions.[/*]
[*][b]Light data:[/b] world position, linear HDR RGB, luminance, recognized spotlight direction/cone and renderer-selection metadata.[/*]
[*][b]Physics collision checks:[/b] clear, blocked or unknown, with ray counts and measurement age. Enabled by default.[/*]
[*][b]Three light feeds:[/b] raw contributions, grouped/smoothed contributions, and a clear-only grouped/smoothed feed. Raw records and RGB are preserved.[/*]
[*][b]Player and camera:[/b] player position/root orientation plus independent native render-camera position, orientation and projection.[/*]
[*][b]Ambient/sky:[/b] global sky RGB, optional camera-local sky visibility and a derived local ambient estimate. Local fields can be null.[/*]
[*][b]HUD and API:[/b] fullscreen markers, player-centered 3D radar, notices, local HTTP snapshots/health/schema and WebSocket streams.[/*]
[*][b]SDR/HDR UI:[/b] SDR, HDR10 and scRGB support. Automated rendering tests are separate from live display/game acceptance.[/*]
[/list]

The 2.2.4 sky-readback and world-entry queue corrections remain included.

