[size=4][b]Important visibility limitation[/b][/size]
The mod casts nine physics rays from the [b]camera position[/b] toward points around each nearby source, including behind the camera.
[list]
[*][b]Clear:[/b] at least one sampled path is unobstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Blocked:[/b] all nine paths are obstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Unknown:[/b] missing, invalid, incomplete or older than 500 ms.[/*]
[/list]
[b]This measures collision, not optical visibility or visible brightness.[/b] Dense cages or simplified closed collision hulls can mark visibly shining lamps blocked. Colliding glass is not reliably distinguished from opaque geometry, and geometry without suitable collision may fail to block. More rays alone do not resolve these differences. Renderer selection is not proof that a light is optically visible. Renderer-derived contribution research is unfinished and is not a release feature.

By default, blocked markers are dimmed and unknown sources remain visible. [b]F11[/b] shows only fresh clear lights in both marker and radar views. The clear-only API feed and current Hue consumer exclude blocked/unknown sources before weighting; increasing a type weight cannot recover them.

The shared radius defaults to [b]100 game units around the player[/b], configurable through LightOverlay/Radius. Up to 256 targets share a bounded query budget; crowded scenes may refresh individual lights less often.

The raw and unfiltered smoothed streams stay complete. The separate clear-only feed filters before grouping/smoothing:
[code]
GET /v1/lights/visible
WS  /v1/lights/visible/stream[/code]
If measurements are unavailable, the feed reports unavailable. Setting [SourceVisibility] Enabled=0 leaves raw data available but provides no measured clear-only output.

[size=4][b]Install or upgrade[/b][/size]
Requires:
[list]
[*]Windows x64 and Crimson Desert patch [b]2.03.02[/b] on Steam.[/*]
[*]Microsoft [b].NET 8 ASP.NET Core Runtime (x64)[/b].[/*]
[*]One compatible x64 ASI loader installed for the game.[/*]
[/list]
[list]
[*]Close the game before installing, updating or removing the mod.[/*]
[*]Back up useful INI preferences. Disable/remove older Telemetry packages and any separate legacy console ASI.[/*]
[*]Import and [b]activate/deploy[/b] the complete CrimsonDesertTelemetry-v2.2.5-ModManagers.zip. Importing alone is insufficient.[/*]
[*]Keep all six runtime files from the same version together beside the game EXE.[/*]
[*]Merge preferences into the supplied INI, launch the game and load a save.[/*]
[/list]
[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.asi
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.ini
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg[/code]

[b]DMM upgrade note:[/b] DMM 2.8.1/3.1.1 can leave both DLL and both lowercase CFG companions behind after removal. With the game closed and the old package removed, delete only stale Telemetry companions before redeploying. Preserve your ASI loader and customized INI. Keep .cfg names unchanged; loose .json files can be interpreted as game patches.

[b]Optional Hue:[/b] install [b]CrimsonHue 1.0.0 separately[/b], plus Microsoft [b].NET 8 Desktop Runtime (x64)[/b]. Use the matching release pair. CDT does not require Hue hardware or the Hue controller. Stop Hue sync and wait for stopped status before game exit. Hot unloading is unsupported.

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]F8:[/b] show/hide telemetry HUD and 3D radar.[/*]
[*][b]F9:[/b] toggle details.[/*]
[*][b]F10:[/b] show/hide fullscreen light markers.[/*]
[*][b]F11:[/b] toggle complete / fresh-clear-only light views.[/*]
[*][b]Ctrl+Shift+H:[/b] open/hide the optional Hue mixer; configurable in Hue Setup.[/*]
[/list]
F8-F11 can be reassigned in the INI using decimal Windows virtual-key codes; 0 disables a shortcut. INI changes require restart. Automatic hiding in every game menu is not implemented. [CrimsonHue] Enabled=0 disables optional panel discovery.

[size=4][b]Data and compatibility limits[/b][/size]
Authored/rendered/upstream arrays overlap; do not add them together. The current upstream light input is not a persistent world registry; absence does not prove OFF. Linear HDR RGB is not lumens, final screen pixels or exposure-normalized color. Sun, sky, emissive surfaces and every effect are not all covered. Fast motion can expose capture/projection latency.

No NVIDIA, DLSS, Streamline, Nsight or PIX runtime dependency is required. Live HDR-display, frame-generation and AMD/Intel game acceptance remain limited; automated GPU checks do not establish every setup. The full system uses guarded hooks, GPU copies and physics queries, not purely read-only instrumentation. No gameplay-control API or anti-cheat bypass is provided.

